# Line endings here are per-file history, not a convention. Twelve files are
# CRLF — four in js/, sources.html, the vendored js-yaml, four of the data
# YAMLs and both generated graph files; entities.yaml is mixed; the rest are
# LF. Nothing in the build or the page depends on which, and normalising any
# of it would be one commit touching every line of every file it touched. So
# git is told to convert nothing, in either direction.
#
# This also keeps build/build.mjs honest. It writes data/graph.json and
# data/graph.js with CRLF on purpose, to match the sources they compile from
# (see the comment above `crlf` there). Any rule that normalised those two
# would leave them dirty after every build, with no change in the data.
#
# Note what this does not do: git records whatever bytes a tool writes. A
# script that reads a file and writes it back through something that
# normalises newlines still rewrites every line, and git will commit that
# faithfully. `git diff --stat` after a scripted edit is the check for it.
* -text

# Evidence is bytes. Never convert it, and don't diff it as text.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.webp  binary
*.avif  binary
*.wav   binary
